{
    "title": "Airworthiness Directives; Airbus Helicopters",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 08, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-08/pdf/2025-22279.pdf",
    "summary": "The Federal Aviation Administration has issued two new safety directives for certain Airbus helicopters to prevent cargo from dropping in flight and to address cracking in the tail fin. Both rules take effect January 12, 2026, and aim to reduce risks to crews, passengers, and people on the ground.\n\nOperators of AS350 and AS355 series helicopters, as well as EC130 B4 models, are most affected. One directive calls for a one-time check of the cargo hook\u2019s emergency release control after a reported incident where an incorrectly adjusted cable led to a lost load. If issues are found, operators must adjust the system or replace parts. The other directive replaces an earlier rule that required repeated inspections of the vertical tail fin; it now requires upgrading the upper fin assembly to a new design, which removes the need for those ongoing checks. In the long run, pilots, maintenance teams, and communities benefit from a lower chance of unexpected cargo release or tail damage, and operators gain a clearer, one-and-done solution for the fin issue.\n\nOn the downside, operators face new costs and potential downtime. The agency estimates the inspection for the cargo release control at about $85 per helicopter, with adjustments around $170 if needed, and about $3,655 if a component must be replaced. For the tail fin, the required upgrade will add parts and labor expenses beyond the earlier inspection routine. Smaller operators, in particular, may feel the impact of scheduling, parts availability, and documentation. One commenter also criticized the FAA\u2019s practice of relying on European source documents by reference, arguing it can be confusing; the agency kept that approach, saying it speeds up safety actions and is a standard, lawful method.\n\nIn total, about 1,000 U.S.-registered helicopters fall under the cargo release directive. For both actions, the FAA allows special flights to maintenance locations under certain conditions. While the requirements bring short-term costs, the goal is straightforward: prevent load drops and structural failures before they can happen.",
